program            = 008_inline_asm_per_target
area               = 08_gcc_extensions
description        = GCC inline assembly with operand constraints, guarded per architecture, every branch performing the same 32-bit integer operation
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_audit_flags     = -Wall -Wextra -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror
ub_notes           <<END
UB-GATE DEVIATION AND ITS REASON. Every program in area 08_gcc_extensions drops
-pedantic from the default warning gate. Reason: the constructs under test are
non-standard GCC extensions by definition, and -pedantic exists precisely to reject
them; retaining it would fail every program in the area for doing exactly what it is
meant to do. This is the only area-wide deviation in the corpus. No other warning is
relaxed: -Wall, -Wextra, -Wconversion, -Wsign-conversion, -Wshadow and -Werror are all
retained, and this program is clean under them at -O0, -O1 and -O2. Measured with
gcc 13.3.0.

UB-FREEDOM ARGUMENT. There is no signed overflow: the computations are 1000+37, 1000-37,
37-1000, 1000 xor 37 and -1000 xor 37, all far from INT_MIN and INT_MAX. No shift operator
is used. No pointer, array or aggregate is used, so there is no aliasing violation and no
one-past-end pointer. No uninitialized read occurs: each result variable is either
initialized from the first operand before a read-write "+r" operand is applied, or written
by the assembly through a "=r" output operand before it is returned. No object is modified
twice between sequence points. Every printf call receives at most one side-effecting
argument. Nothing depends on padding bytes or on the addresses of unrelated objects.

NO REGISTER IS CLOBBERED WITHOUT BEING DECLARED. On x86_64 and i386 the addl, subl and
xorl instructions write the condition flags, so those branches declare the "cc" clobber;
omitting it would let the compiler assume the flags survive across the asm and could
produce wrong code. On aarch64 the plain add, sub and eor forms do NOT set flags, only the
adds and subs forms do, and riscv64 has no flags register at all, so neither of those
branches declares "cc", because declaring it there would be incorrect. No general-purpose
register outside the declared operands is touched by any branch.

Every asm carries __volatile__. Each has an output operand, so without __volatile__ the
compiler would be free to delete or hoist the instruction once it proved the result unused
or invariant, which would silently stop the program from testing code generation.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
PER-TARGET ASSEMBLY DESIGN. All four branches perform the SAME logical 32-bit integer
operation and assign the result to int, so every printed value is identical on every
target. Only register constraints are used -- "r", "=r", "+r" and the matching "0" -- and
never an immediate-letter constraint, because immediate constraint letters differ per
architecture and would not port.

  x86_64 and i386   add  "addl %1, %0"          "+r" (r) : "r" (b) : "cc"
                    sub  "subl %2, %0"          "=r" (r) : "0" (a), "r" (b) : "cc"
                    xor  "xorl %1, %0"          "+r" (r) : "r" (b) : "cc"
                    The l mnemonic suffix with an int operand makes %0 expand to the
                    32-bit register name. These instructions write the flags, hence "cc".
                    The sub form uses the MATCHING constraint "0", which ties input a to
                    the same register as output 0; verified in the emitted assembly as
                    subl %eax, %ecx with %ecx serving as both output and matching input.
                    This is the only matching-constraint coverage in the corpus.

  aarch64           add  "add %w0, %w1, %w2"    "=r" (r) : "r" (a), "r" (b)
                    sub  "sub %w0, %w1, %w2"    "=r" (r) : "r" (a), "r" (b)
                    xor  "eor %w0, %w1, %w2"    "=r" (r) : "r" (a), "r" (b)
                    The %w modifier is REQUIRED to name the 32-bit views of the registers;
                    plain %0 would name the 64-bit register and give int-incorrect
                    semantics. No "cc" clobber, because these forms do not set flags.

  riscv64           add  "addw %0, %1, %2"      "=r" (r) : "r" (a), "r" (b)
                    sub  "subw %0, %1, %2"      "=r" (r) : "r" (a), "r" (b)
                    xor  "xor %0, %1, %2"       "=r" (r) : "r" (a), "r" (b)
                    addw and subw are REQUIRED for 32-bit int semantics with correct sign
                    extension into the 64-bit register. xor is deliberately NOT xorw: no
                    such instruction exists, and full-width xor is correct here, because
                    if both operands are sign-extended 32-bit values then the upper 32
                    bits of the result equal sign_a xor sign_b, which is exactly the
                    replicated sign of the low word, so the result is already correctly
                    sign-extended. No "cc" clobber, because riscv64 has no flags register.

NOTHING TARGET-SPECIFIC IS EVER PRINTED. This is the property that keeps oracle b sound.
Oracle b compares each backend's stdout against the x86_64 baseline, so if any branch
computed a different value, or if the program printed the architecture name, a register
name or any other target-varying fact, oracle b would report a divergence caused by the
TEST rather than by the compiler -- which would look exactly like a real defect. The line
asm_branch_selected therefore prints the constant 1, meaning only that exactly one branch
was selected, which the if/elif/else structure guarantees.

Every operand and result is int, which is 4 bytes on all four targets. long is
deliberately avoided: it was measured as 4 bytes on i686 and 8 bytes on x86_64, aarch64
and riscv64. Plain char is never used, so the measured char-signedness difference cannot be
reached: char is signed on x86_64 and i686 but unsigned on aarch64 and riscv64. No address
or pointer value is printed.

THE ELSE BRANCH DELIBERATELY RAISES A LOUD #error AND MUST NEVER BECOME A PORTABLE-C
FALLBACK. The repository documentation contains zero mentions of predefined architecture
macros: a grep across docs/ for __x86_64__, __i386__, __aarch64__, __riscv, __STDC__ and
_LP64 returns nothing, so whether bcc defines them is unverified from the documentation. A
portable-C fallback would silently substitute plain C arithmetic for the assembly, every
value would still be correct, every oracle would report PASS, and the program would have
stopped testing inline assembly entirely, invisibly and permanently. The loud #error
instead diagnoses PREDEFINED-MACRO SUPPORT, which is itself worth knowing, and it makes
the failure impossible to miss. Verified with gcc 13.3.0: compiling with -U__x86_64__
-U__amd64__ fires the #error as intended. Each guard also accepts alternate spellings --
__x86_64__ or __amd64__, __i386__ or __i386, __aarch64__ or __arm64__, __riscv or
__riscv__ -- as resilience against a compiler that defines only one spelling of a pair.
Note that the plan places a dedicated predefined-macro program in area 12, so the plan does
assume these macros are defined.

asm_matches_c IS A GENUINE INDEPENDENT CROSS-CHECK, not decoration. It compares every
assembly result against the same computation written in plain C inside the SAME binary,
which catches the one case the three oracles cannot: if bcc and the reference compiler
mis-assembled identically, oracle a would report agreement and oracle c would match a
golden record captured from the same bug. If that line ever prints 0, the assembly
disagrees with plain C in the same binary and there is a real defect regardless of what the
other oracles say.

The folded variant uses literal arguments the optimizer can fold through, while the runtime
variant and asm_chain are driven by volatile int operands so the backend must emit genuine
instructions; asm_chain additionally nests three asm calls, forcing register allocation
across them.

Inline assembly with operand constraints is documented as supported at
docs/technical-specifications.md lines 13, 107 and 761, with the spellings asm and
__asm__, and at docs/project-guide.md lines 81 and 206. This program therefore tests
documented functionality and carries NO expected-divergence marker.

Output is identical on all four targets at all three optimization levels.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
asm_branch_selected=1
asm_folded_add=1037
asm_folded_sub=963
asm_folded_xor=973
asm_folded_sub_neg=-963
asm_folded_xor_neg=-963
asm_runtime_add=1037
asm_runtime_sub=963
asm_runtime_xor=973
asm_runtime_sub_neg=-963
asm_runtime_xor_neg=-963
asm_chain=1936
asm_matches_c=1
END
